course: everyday
lesson: 397
title: Whose turn is it?
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At Mia's house the hardest level of any game was not on the screen; it was deciding whose turn it was. Kai kept saying «one more life» and had been on the controller for forty minutes. Everyone else was bored and cross, but Kai honestly thought it had been ten minutes, because time flies when you are playing and crawls when you are waiting. Priya put a timer on her phone for each turn, and the arguing stopped, because now the timer was the bad guy, not a person.
explain: There were three kids and two controllers, and Zara always got the spare one that stuck. Then Noah announced «winner stays on», which sounded fair, except Noah was the best, so he never got off. Mia changed it to «loser stays on» for Saturdays, they swapped the sticky controller every match, and Noah had to learn to lose with a smile.
explain: When Ruby went to the toilet, Leo grabbed her controller: «You left, so it's mine.» Mia ruled that a toilet break is not giving up your turn, and they paused the game until Ruby came back. Leo groaned but agreed. Then he said guests should get longer turns; Mia's mum said guests may go first, but every turn stays the same length. Priya and Theo both shouted that they were «next», so they did rock, paper, scissors, best of three, and the loser picked the next game. At a party, Ollie wanted to play the long story mode on his own while eight kids watched; they agreed story mode is for home and party games are for parties.
explain: Kai's little sister wanted a go, so the big kids gave her an unplugged controller. She was thrilled for ten minutes, then worked it out, burst into tears, and Kai's mum made him give her a real turn. Zara kept letting everyone go first and then never got a turn at all, until Mia said «Zara's been waiting longest, she's on», and Zara beamed. Jayden made a turn chart in four colours and stuck it on Mia's wall. Everybody laughed at him for being so organised, and it stayed up for months as the only thing nobody argued about.
fact: Time feels shorter when you are playing and longer when you are waiting.
fact: A timer makes turns fair without anyone having to be the bad guy.
fact: Fairness includes sharing the broken or boring things, not only the fun ones.
fact: A rule that only ever helps the strongest player is not a fair rule.
fact: A short break, like going to the toilet, does not mean giving up your turn.
fact: When two people are both sure they are next, a quick fair way like rock, paper, scissors settles it faster than shouting.
fact: Games where everyone can join are better for parties than long single-player games.
fact: Tricking a trusting little child stops being funny when they find out.
fact: Being kind does not mean never asking for your own turn.
fact: Rules written down, like a turn chart, are easier to keep.
fact: A guest can be allowed to go first without getting a longer turn than everyone else.
exercise: Why did Kai think he had only played for ten minutes? | Time feels shorter when you are playing | His watch was ten minutes slow all afternoon | The game had a timer that lied | Someone else had been playing too
exercise: How did the others feel while Kai kept saying «one more life»? | Bored and cross | Happy to watch him win | Sleepy and calm on the sofa | Proud of how good he was
exercise: Why did Priya's phone timer stop the arguing? | The timer was the bad guy, not a person | Kai was scared of the phone | The phone switched the console off by itself | Priya got the most turns that way
exercise: Why was «winner stays on» unfair at Mia's house? | Noah was the best, so the others hardly played | Noah always cheated in every game they played | Winners should never be allowed to play | It made the game go too fast
exercise: What did they do about the sticky spare controller? | Swapped it every match so everyone had a go | Threw it away and bought a brand new one | Gave it to Zara because she was the guest | Let the loser keep it forever
exercise: Leo grabbed Ruby's controller while she was in the toilet. What did Mia rule? | A toilet break is not giving up your turn | Whoever is holding the controller gets to keep it | Ruby should have taken it with her | Leo gets two turns as a punishment
exercise: What could they have done instead of grabbing when Ruby left? | Paused the game until she came back | Turned the console off and on again | Started a new game without her | Hidden Ruby's controller from her
exercise: How did Kai's little sister feel when she found out the controller was unplugged? | Tricked and upset | Proud that she was so good | Happy because it was a joke | Bored and ready for bed
exercise: Priya and Theo both said they were next. What settled it? | Rock, paper, scissors, best of three | Whoever shouted loudest | Mia's dog chose one of them | They both played with one controller
exercise: Why was story mode a poor choice at the party? | Everyone else had to sit and watch | Story mode is not allowed at parties | It makes the console overheat | Party guests cannot read the words
exercise: Zara let others go first and never got a turn. What is the lesson? | You can be kind and still say «my go now» | Being kind always gets you the most turns | Kind people should not play games | Zara should have grabbed a controller
exercise: How did Zara feel when Mia said «she's on»? | She beamed | She felt embarrassed and left | She said she did not want it | She was cross with Mia
exercise: What happened to Jayden's turn chart? | It stayed up for months and nobody argued about it | Theo tore it down the same day | Mia's mum said it had to go | It caused the biggest argument of all
exercise: Leo said guests should get longer turns. What did Mia's mum say? | Guests go first, but turns stay the same length | Guests get double turns at every house they visit | Hosts must always let guests win | Guests should wait until last
exercise: Which is the fairest rule when the best player keeps winning? | Loser stays on, so everyone gets to play | Winner stays on, as the best deserves it | Only the owner of the console plays | Whoever is oldest decides who plays
test: «one more life, swear down, just ONE more» — it's been 40 mins. What's the fair fix? | A timer for every turn | Let him finish the whole game | Unplug the telly on him | Take the controller and run
test: «you went to the loo so it's my controller now innit». Fair? | No, a short break doesn't lose your turn | Yes, you snooze you lose | Yes, if you were gone more than a minute | Only if the toilet is upstairs
test: «I was next!» «NO I was!!» shouting for five minutes. Quickest fair fix? | Rock, paper, scissors | Whoever's mum is nicer | Shout louder until one gives up | Nobody plays, game over
test: «give the baby the dead controller, she won't know lol». What happens next most likely? | She finds out, and it stops being funny | She never notices, so it's fine and nobody's hurt | She becomes a pro at the game | Mum says well done for sharing
test: «winner stays on, sorry not sorry» says the best player. What's wrong with it? | The others hardly ever get to play | Winners always get bored quickly and stop on their own | It makes the game too easy | It breaks the console's rules
test: Ten kids at a party, Ollie: «I'm doing the story mode, it's only 2 hours». Best answer? | Play something everyone can join | Fine, we'll watch, it's his turn | Let him play while the rest go home | Let him play it but turn the sound off
test: «Zara, you go last again yeah? you don't mind». Zara's waited all day. What should she say? | «Actually it's my go now» | «Fine, I'll never play» | «Okay, I'll just watch again» | «You're all mean, I'm going home»
test: «a turn chart?? are you a teacher or what» — Jayden's chart. Why does it work? | Rules written down are easier to keep | Teachers like charts on walls | The bright colours on it make people feel calm | Nobody can read the names on it
test: Leo: «I'm the guest, so I get longer». Mia's mum is right that…? | Guests can go first, but turns are equal | Guests always get the longest turns, that's polite | Hosts should never get a turn | Only grown-ups can make turn rules
test: Three kids, two controllers, one sticks. «Zara's the slowest so she gets the sticky one». Fair rule? | Swap the sticky one every match | The slowest player keeps it till the end | The oldest player picks first | The owner gets both good ones
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a child engaged in play perceives a lengthy turn as brief. | Time is perceived as passing more quickly during enjoyable activity | The clocks in the household were inaccurate | The game had deliberately misreported the time to the players | The child was attempting to deceive the others
exam: It is given that one participant departs temporarily to use the lavatory. Select the response consistent with the established rules concerning the participant's turn. | The turn is retained, and the game is paused | The turn is forfeited to whoever seizes the controller | The turn is transferred to the host | The turn is cancelled and the game restarted
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the rule «winner stays on» where one participant is markedly more skilled than the rest? | It is unfair, as the remaining participants seldom play | It is fair, as merit should be rewarded without limit | It is fair provided the host approves of it | It is unfair only when the winner is a guest
exam: State the method recommended for the resolution of a dispute wherein two participants each assert entitlement to the subsequent turn. | A brief impartial procedure such as rock, paper, scissors | Prolonged argument until one party concedes | The removal of both parties from the room | The award of the turn to the louder party
